{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Uy9iUbobRxKnzbPEaEfEwL","type":"tool_result","content":"# Verbatim Extracts from Kubernetes Jobs Documentation\n\n## 1. Full Opening Definition\n\n\"A Job creates one or more Pods and will continue to retry execution of the Pods until a specified number of them successfully terminate. As pods successfully complete, the Job tracks the successful completions. When a specified number of successful completions is reached, the task (ie, Job) is complete. Deleting a Job will clean up the Pods it created. Suspending a Job will delete its active Pods until the Job is resumed again.\"\n\n## 2. Reliable Pod Completion and Parallel Processing\n\n\"A simple case is to create one Job object in order to reliably run one Pod to completion. The Job object will start a new Pod if the first Pod fails or is deleted (for example due to a node hardware failure or a node reboot).\n\nYou can also use a Job to run multiple Pods in parallel.\"\n\n## 3. Completion Mode Section\n\nThe page content provided does not include the complete \"Completion mode\" section with full descriptions of NonIndexed and Indexed modes. The document is truncated at that point.\n\n## 4. Parallel Execution for Jobs\n\n\"There are three main types of task suitable to run as a Job:\n\n1. Non-parallel Jobs\n   - normally, only one Pod is started, unless the Pod fails.\n   - the Job is complete as soon as its Pod terminates successfully.\n2. Parallel Jobs with a _fixed completion count_:\n   - specify a non-zero positive value for `\"\n\n[The section is truncated in the provided content]\n\n## 5. Handling Pod and Container Failures\n\nThe specific section on container failures and restartPolicy is not included in the provided excerpts before the content truncation occurs.\n\n## 6. Pod Backoff Failure Policy\n\nThe `.spec.backoffLimit` section is not fully provided in the truncated content.\n\n## 7. Job Termination and Cleanup\n\nInformation ab